Title: "Resource Headers"
Id: "0eabf9e6-6eaa-4ca5-ae55-da8d56eda95c"

Text: "Resource headers begin with `$` and are used by `Resource:` groups and `References:` sections.

```mlg-fragment
[$zfc.foundations]
Resource:
. title: \"ZFC Foundations\"
. url: \"https://example.com\"
```

A `References:` section names resources with these headers. Both the bare
`$book.chapter` form and the quoted `\"$book.chapter\"` form are accepted. Entries may be tagged with a reference label using `. [label]: $book.chapter`:

```mlg-fragment
References:
. [foundations]: $zfc.foundations:page{42}
. [analysis]: $rudin.principles
```

Duplicate reference labels within the same `References:` section are rejected.

Formulations and prose can cite declared reference labels:

- In formulations, append `(:label:)` or `(:ref1, ref2:)` to a parenthesized expression or statement, optionally combined with justification labels: `(x = x)(:analysis:)` or `(x = x)[:1:](:analysis:)`.
- In prose (`OpenText`), cite sources using &#39;&#39;quoted text&#39;&#39;(&#58;label&#58;) or &#39;&#39;quoted text&#39;&#39;(&#58;ref1, ref2&#58;).

Every cited reference label must match a labeled entry in the enclosing item's `References:` section; citing an unknown label produces an error.

The resource's `offset:` is the physical PDF page on which its logical page 1
appears, so the linked physical page is `n + offset - 1` (`offset` defaults to
`1`).

The viewer displays a resolved reference as the resource's title followed by its
author entries in parentheses, linking to the resource's `url:` when it has one.
Citations in prose and formulations render as interactive hyperlinks to the referenced resource.
`References:` is supporting content and is collapsed by default alongside
`Documented:` and `Id:`."
Id: "4a1ddf88-4cdb-4ef9-a0e8-c71830b26f68"
